What to Eat During Your Period: Simple Foods for Better Comfort

Periods can affect your appetite, energy levels, mood, and overall comfort. During these few days, choosing nourishing foods can be a simple way to support your body. You do not need a complicated diet or expensive ingredients. Everyday foods such as fruits, vegetables, whole grains, nuts, seeds, and protein-rich meals can fit easily into your routine.

One useful place to start is with iron-rich foods. Menstrual bleeding means your body loses some iron, so including foods such as spinach, lentils, beans, chickpeas, peas, tofu, eggs, and lean meats can be helpful as part of a balanced diet. Pairing plant-based iron sources with vitamin C-rich foods such as oranges, guava, tomatoes, or bell peppers can also support iron absorption.

Magnesium-containing foods may also be a good addition to your meals. Nuts, seeds, whole grains, dark leafy vegetables, and legumes provide magnesium along with other nutrients. A small handful of almonds or pumpkin seeds can make an easy snack when you feel hungry between meals.

Fruits are another convenient option during your period. Bananas, oranges, apples, watermelon, papaya, and berries can provide fluids, carbohydrates, vitamins, and minerals. If your appetite is low, a bowl of fruit or a simple smoothie may feel easier than a heavy meal.

Hydration matters too. Water should be your main drink throughout the day. Coconut water, soups, and water-rich fruits can also contribute to your fluid intake. Some people find that limiting excessive caffeine and very sugary drinks helps them feel more comfortable during their period.

Comfort food does not have to mean unhealthy food. A warm bowl of vegetable soup, dal with rice, oatmeal with banana, or a homemade vegetable sandwich can be satisfying while still providing useful nutrients.

It is also completely okay to enjoy a favourite treat. Chocolate cravings are common around periods, and you do not have to completely avoid foods you enjoy. The goal is balance rather than restriction.

